"""
# Cross-module enum discriminant comparison Specification

**Feature IDs:** N/A
**Category:** Runtime
**Difficulty:** 3/5
**Status:** Fixed
**Requirements:** N/A
**Plan:** N/A
**Design:** N/A
**Research:** doc/08_tracking/bug/interpreter_cross_module_enum_discriminant_3_compares_false_2026-08-04.md

## Overview

`interpreter_cross_module_enum_discriminant_3_compares_false_2026-08-04`
reported that a payload-less enum value read out of a struct field compared
**false** against its own literal under the tree-walk interpreter — but only
for the variant at **discriminant 3**, and only when the enum was declared in a
different module. Discriminant 3 is the runtime's nil sentinel, so the
importing and defining sides disagreed on the encoding for exactly that
variant. The JIT was correct, which is what made the divergence silent.

The record names `std.ndarray`'s `DType` (`F32`=0, `F64`=1, `I64`=2, `Bool`=3)
as the reproducer and attributes 7 `test/03_system/feature/scilib` failures to
it. This spec uses that same real enum — a locally declared enum of identical
shape compared correctly even while the bug was live, so a same-file guard
would be vacuous for this defect.

Re-checked 2026-09-12: every discriminant compares correctly on both lanes.

## Syntax

```simple
val a = array_bool([Bool.new(true)])
a.dtype == DType.Bool      # discriminant 3 — must be true
```

"""

use std.spec
use std.ndarray.*


# ============================================================================
# Test Group 1: every discriminant, positive comparison
# ============================================================================

describe "cross-module DType equality by discriminant":
    """
    ## 0, 1, 2, 3

    Discriminants 0-2 were always correct; 3 is the defect. All four are
    asserted so a future shift of the sentinel to another slot is visible.
    """

    it "compares discriminant 0 (F32) equal":
        val a = array_f32([Float32.new(1.0)])
        expect(a.dtype == DType.F32).to_equal(true)

    it "compares discriminant 1 (F64) equal":
        val a = array([Float64.new(1.0)])
        expect(a.dtype == DType.F64).to_equal(true)

    it "compares discriminant 2 (I64) equal":
        val a = array_i64([Int64.new(1)])
        expect(a.dtype == DType.I64).to_equal(true)

    it "compares discriminant 3 (Bool) equal — the defect":
        val a = array_bool([Bool.new(true)])
        expect(a.dtype == DType.Bool).to_equal(true)


# ============================================================================
# Test Group 2: negative comparisons and the != guard shape
# ============================================================================

describe "cross-module DType inequality":
    """
    ## The guard shape

    The 7 scilib failures all bottomed out in a `dtype == DType.Bool` /
    `dtype != DType.Bool` guard, so both polarities are pinned. A defect that
    made discriminant 3 compare false would flip the `!=` guard true and take
    the wrong branch silently.
    """

    it "reports discriminant 3 as not equal to another variant":
        val a = array_bool([Bool.new(true)])
        expect(a.dtype == DType.I64).to_equal(false)

    it "reports a non-Bool dtype as not equal to Bool":
        val a = array_i64([Int64.new(1)])
        expect(a.dtype != DType.Bool).to_equal(true)

    it "reports Bool as not not-equal to Bool":
        val a = array_bool([Bool.new(true)])
        expect(a.dtype != DType.Bool).to_equal(false)
